    <node id="Paper_1216">
      <data key="d0">Paper</data>
      <data key="d1">InfoVis</data>
      <data key="d2">1998</data>
      <data key="d3">The generalized detail in-context problem</data>
      <data key="d4">10.1109/infvis.1998.729558</data>
      <data key="d5">http://dx.doi.org/10.1109/INFVIS.1998.729558</data>
      <data key="d6">44.0</data>
      <data key="d7" />
      <data key="d8">C</data>
      <data key="d9">The paper describes a general formulation of the "detail-in-context" problem, which is a central issue of fundamental importance to a wide variety of nonlinear magnification systems. A number of tools are described for dealing with this problem effectively. These tools can be applied to any continuous nonlinear magnification system, and are not tied to specific implementation features of the system that produced the original transformation. Of particular interest is the development of "seamless multi level views", which allow multiple global views of an information space (each having different information content) to be integrated into a single view without discontinuity.</data>
      <data key="d10">Alan Keahey</data>
      <data key="d11">T.A. Keahey</data>
      <data key="d12">Los Alamos National Laboratory, Los Alamos, NM, USA</data>
      <data key="d13">10.1109/infvis.1997.636786;10.1109/infvis.1997.636718;10.1109/infvis.1996.559214;10.1109/infvis.1997.636786</data>
      <data key="d14" />
      <data key="d15">25.0</data>
      <data key="d16">30.0</data>
      <data key="d17">25.0</data>
      <data key="d18">176.0</data>
      <data key="d19" />
      <data key="d20" />
    </node>
    <node id="Paper_1226">
      <data key="d0">Paper</data>
      <data key="d1">InfoVis</data>
      <data key="d2">2020</data>
      <data key="d3">Context-aware Sampling of Large Networks via Graph Representation Learning</data>
      <data key="d4">10.1109/tvcg.2020.3030440</data>
      <data key="d5">http://dx.doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2020.3030440</data>
      <data key="d6">1709.0</data>
      <data key="d7">1719.0</data>
      <data key="d8">J</data>
      <data key="d9">Numerous sampling strategies have been proposed to simplify large-scale networks for highly readable visualizations. It is of great challenge to preserve contextual structures formed by nodes and edges with tight relationships in a sampled graph, because they are easily overlooked during the process of sampling due to their irregular distribution and immunity to scale. In this paper, a new graph sampling method is proposed oriented to the preservation of contextual structures. We first utilize a graph representation learning (GRL) model to transform nodes into vectors so that the contextual structures in a network can be effectively extracted and organized. Then, we propose a multi-objective blue noise sampling model to select a subset of nodes in the vectorized space to preserve contextual structures with the retention of relative data and cluster densities in addition to those features of significance, such as bridging nodes and graph connections. We also design a set of visual interfaces enabling users to interactively conduct context-aware sampling, visually compare results with various sampling strategies, and deeply explore large networks. Case studies and quantitative comparisons based on real-world datasets have demonstrated the effectiveness of our method in the abstraction and exploration of large networks.</data>
      <data key="d10">Zhiguang Zhou;Chen Shi;Xilong Shen;Lihong Cai;Haoxuan Wang;Yuhua Liu;Ying Zhao 0001;Wei Chen 0001</data>
      <data key="d11">Zhiguang Zhou;Chen Shi;Xilong Shen;Lihong Cai;Haoxuan Wang;Yuhua Liu;Ying Zhao;Wei Chen</data>
      <data key="d12">School of Information, Zhejiang University of Finance and Economics;School of Information, Zhejiang University of Finance and Economics;School of Information, Zhejiang University of Finance and Economics;School of Information, Zhejiang University of Finance and Economics;School of Information, Zhejiang University of Finance and Economics;School of Information, Zhejiang University of Finance and Economics;Central South University;State Key Lab of CAD &amp; CG, Zhejiang University</data>
      <data key="d13">10.1109/tvcg.2006.120;10.1109/tvcg.2018.2865139;10.1109/tvcg.2008.135;10.1109/infvis.2004.1;10.1109/tvcg.2006.147;10.1109/tvcg.2008.151;10.1109/tvcg.2017.2743858;10.1109/tvcg.2017.2744938;10.1109/tvcg.2016.2598831;10.1109/tvcg.2015.2468078;10.1109/tvcg.2015.2467691;10.1109/tvcg.2016.2598867;10.1109/tvcg.2018.2865020;10.1109/tvcg.2018.2864503;10.1109/tvcg.2012.238;10.1109/tvcg.2006.120</data>
      <data key="d14">Graph sampling,Graph representation learning,Blue noise sampling,Graph evaluation</data>
      <data key="d15">20.0</data>
      <data key="d16">29.0</data>
      <data key="d17">58.0</data>
      <data key="d18">1085.0</data>
      <data key="d19" />
      <data key="d20" />
